## Score Descriptions:

### Sensitivity:



::: {.cell}

:::

::: {.cell layout-align="center" lightbox='true'}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Black sea bass Sensitivity on the Northeast Shelf](../figures/Black sea bass/Blackseabass_sensitivity_2.0.pdf){#fig-sens-bsb fig-align='center' fig-pos='H' width=85%}
:::
:::



Black Sea Bass received a **Low** sensitivity score (@fig-sum-tab-bsb). Certainty was 64%. The only score greater than 2.5 was Complexity in Reproductive Strategy (2.7). This is likely because black sea bass as protogynous hermaphrodites (they transition from female to male as they age). Black Sea Bass also spawn in aggregations that consist of a single dominant male and many females [@tabandera2024black].

In the 2016 Climate Vulnerability Assessment, Black Sea Bass received a Moderate sensitivity score. This was driven by Complexity in Reproductive Strategy (which received the same score in both assessments) and Early Life History Requirements [@hare2016vulnerability]. Juvenile Habitat Requirements was likely important because Black Sea Bass rely on seagrasses and shellfish beds as nursery habitats [@guyant2022northeast]. This attribute received a score of 2.4 in our assessment.

### Exposure:



::: {.cell}

:::

::: {.cell layout-align="center" lightbox='true'}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Maps of each Exposure Factor for Black sea bass. Exposure was calculated between 2014-2023 and 2025-2035](../figures/Black sea bass/variable_exposure_maps_r20250925_i202501_r20250715_20142023.pdf){#fig-fact-exp-bsb fig-align='center' fig-pos='H' width=85%}
:::
:::



Mean Exposure for Black Sea Bass was **Moderate** throughout its range (2.02 +/- 0.99) (@fig-sum-tab-bsb). In the northern half of its range, exposure was High (2.61 +/- 0.83) whereas it remained Moderate in the southern half of its range (1.5 +/- 0.80) (@fig-sum-tab-bsb). Exposure was greatest south of Cape Cod and remained high south in Southern New England (@fig-exp-bsb). This was driven by high exposure to changes in bottom temperature and oxygen (@fig-fact-exp-bsb). Exposure was Low south of New Jersey, with the exception of the continental shelf, driven by high exposure to changes in bottom aragonite solubility along the shelf break (@fig-exp-bsb, @fig-fact-exp-bsb).

In the previous Assessment, Black Sea Bass received a Very High Exposure score, driven by high exposure to sea surface temperature, air temperature, and ocean acidification [@hare2016vulnerability].



