### Exposure due to Significant Factors



::: {.cell}

:::

::: {.cell layout-align="center" lightbox='true'}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Dynamic Factors driving Black sea bass distributions. The closer the polygon is to the edge of the figure, the more important the variable is. Each ring represents 10% variance explained. Factors must explain at least 10% of variance in the ensemble model to be used to calculate total exposure.](../figures/Black sea bass/dynamic_variable_importance_r20250715.pdf){#fig-radar-bsb fig-align='center' fig-pos='H' width=85%}
:::
:::



Bottom temperature, oxygen, and aragonite solubility were important dynamic drivers of species distributions (@fig-radar-bsb). Temperature is a known driver of larval development and migration timing [@able1995early]. Bivalve beds are important settling habitats, which may drive the importance of aragonite solubility to species distributions [@able1995early]. 

When only these important factors were used to calculate exposure, scores remained Moderate on average throughout the species range (1.72 +/- 0.92). However, scores decreased from High to Moderate (2.28 +/- 0.92) and Moderate to Low (1.22 +/- 0.58) in the northern and southern portions of their range, respectively (@fig-sum-imp-bsb). When exposure calculations were limited to only important factors, exposure was greatest south of Long Island and Cape Cod, driven by high temperature and oxygen exposure (@fig-exp-imp-bsb).



